## Why This Is Asked

Tests branchless bit-twiddling and cross-half reasoning on a 128-bit type. Parity is a classic low-level primitive with real-world use in checksums and error-detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless parity computation
- XOR folding techniques across 64-bit halves
- 128-bit struct handling without loops

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo ^= lo >> 32;
  lo ^= lo >> 16;
  lo ^= lo >> 8;
  lo ^= lo >> 4;
  lo ^= lo >> 2;
  lo ^= lo >> 1;
  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi ^= hi >> 32;
  hi ^= hi >> 16;
  hi ^= hi >> 8;
  hi ^= hi >> 4;
  hi ^= hi >> 2;
  hi ^= hi >> 1;
  return (lo & 1) ^ (hi & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to count parity modulo any odd number (e.g., 3) with similar tricks?
- Could you prove the branchless reduction yields correct parity for all 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests compact, branch-free parity calculation using classic bit-twiddling techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parity calculation through XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts and XOR operations
- Elimination of loops and conditional branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity32(n) {
  n ^= n >>> 16;
  n ^= n >>> 8;
  n ^= n >>> 4;
  n ^= n >>> 2;
  n ^= n >>> 1;
  return n & 1;
}
```

